To make an HTTP request in JavaScript, you can use the built-in `XMLHttpRequest` object or the newer `fetch()` function.

Here's an example using `XMLHttpRequest`:
```javascript
const request = new XMLHttpRequest();
request.open('GET', 'https://example.com/data.json');
request.responseType = 'json';
request.send();

request.onload = function() {
  if (request.status === 200) {
    console.log(request.response);
  } else {
    console.log('Error', request.statusText);
  }
};
```
And here's an example using `fetch()`:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com/data.json')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
Both of these methods make a GET request to the specified URL and retrieve the response data in JSON format. You can modify the request method and headers to make POST requests or include additional data in the request body.

In Rust, you can use the `reqwest` crate to make HTTP requests. Here's an example that sends a GET request and prints the response body:
```rust
use reqwest::blocking::get;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let url = "https://example.com/data.json";
    let response = get(url)?.text()?;
    println!("{}", response);
    Ok(())
}
```
In this example, we use the `get()` function from the `blocking` module to send a GET request to the specified URL. The `text()` function returns the response body as a string. Finally, we print the response body to the console.

You can also modify the request to include headers or data in the request body. Here's an example that sends a POST request with a JSON payload:
```rust
use reqwest::blocking::Client;
use serde_json::json;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let client = Client::new();
    let url = "https://example.com/api/data";
    let payload = json!({
        "key1": "value1",
        "key2": "value2"
    });
    let response = client.post(url)
        .json(&payload)
        .send()?
        .text()?;
    println!("{}", response);
    Ok(())
}
```
In this example, we create a new `Client` instance and use its `post()` method to send a POST request to the specified URL. We use the `json!()` macro from the `serde_json` crate to create a JSON payload, and we use the `json()` method to set the request body to the payload. Finally, we send the request and print the response body to the console.

