Both `v-if` and `v-show` are Vue directives used to conditionally render elements in a Vue template, but they have some differences in how they work.

The main difference between `v-if` and `v-show` is how they handle the element's presence in the DOM. When you use `v-if`, Vue will only render the element if the condition is true, and it will remove it from the DOM if the condition is false. This means that the element is not present in the DOM at all when the condition is false.

On the other hand, when you use `v-show`, Vue will always render the element, but it will only toggle its visibility based on the condition. This means that the element is always present in the DOM, even when the condition is false, but its CSS `display` property is set to "none" to hide it.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:
```php
<template>
  <div>
    <h1 v-if="showTitle">This is a title using v-if</h1>
    <h1 v-show="showTitle">This is a title using v-show</h1>
    <button @click="toggleTitle">Toggle title</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      showTitle: true
    }
  },
  methods: {
    toggleTitle() {
      this.showTitle = !this.showTitle
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, both `v-if` and `v-show` are used to conditionally render a title element based on the value of `showTitle`. The `toggleTitle` method is used to toggle the value of `showTitle`.

If you look at the resulting DOM using the browser's dev tools, you will see that when `v-if` is used, the `h1` element is removed from the DOM when the condition is false. On the other hand, when `v-show` is used, the `h1` element is always present in the DOM, but its `display` property is toggled between "none" and "block" to show or hide it.

