```
The way the function works is that it recursively calls itself on a smaller and smaller subset of the input array, until it reaches the base case where the input array has only one element. At that point, it simply returns an array containing that one element.

For larger input arrays, the function loops through each element and recursively calls itself on the remaining elements, then concatenates the current element to each of the sub-sequences returned by the recursive call. It keeps doing this until all possible combinations have been generated, then returns the array of sequences.

To exclude sequences that are either rotational shifts or reverse order of each other, you can modify the `findSequences` function to use a set to keep track of the sequences that have already been generated. Then, before adding a new sequence to the result array, you can check if it is a rotational shift or reverse order of an existing sequence in the set, and skip it if it is.

Here's the modified implementation:
```javascript
function findSequences(arr) {
  if (arr.length === 1) {
    return [arr];
  }

  const sequences = new Set();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const remaining = arr.slice(0, i).concat(arr.slice(i + 1));
    const subSequences = findSequences(remaining);

    for (let j = 0; j < subSequences.length; j++) {
      const sequence = [arr[i]].concat(subSequences[j]);
      const rotatedSequences = rotateSequence(sequence);
      const reversedSequence = reverseSequence(sequence);

      // check if sequence or any of its rotational shifts or reverse order is already in the set
      if (!sequences.has(sequence) && !rotatedSequences.some(s => sequences.has(s)) && !sequences.has(reversedSequence)) {
        sequences.add(sequence);
      }
    }
  }

  return Array.from(sequences);
}

function rotateSequence(sequence) {
  const rotations = [];

  for (let i = 1; i < sequence.length; i++) {
    const rotated = sequence.slice(i).concat(sequence.slice(0, i));
    rotations.push(rotated);
  }

  return rotations;
}

function reverseSequence(sequence) {
  return sequence.slice().reverse();
}
```
The `findSequences` function now uses a `Set` to store the unique sequences, and it checks each new sequence generated against the existing sequences in the set and their rotational shifts and reverse order. The `rotateSequence` function generates all possible rotational shifts of a sequence by rotating it one element at a time, and the `reverseSequence` function simply returns the reverse order of a sequence.

With these modifications, the `findSequences` function will return an array of arrays containing all unique sequences of the input numbers that are not rotational shifts or reverse order of each other.

Note that this implementation still generates all possible sequences and then filters out the duplicates, so it is not as efficient as it could be. However, for small input sizes like N=4, the performance should still be reasonable. If you need to handle larger input sizes, you may want to consider a different algorithm that generates only the unique sequences directly, rather than filtering duplicates afterwards.

