To make an HTTP request in JavaScript, you can use the `XMLHttpRequest` object or the `fetch()` function.

Here's an example of using `XMLHttpRequest` to make a GET request to retrieve data from a server:
```
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();

xhr.open('GET', 'https://example.com/api/data');

xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(xhr.response); // Success!
  } else {
    console.log('Request failed.  Returned status of ' + xhr.status);
  }
};

xhr.send();
```
Here's an example of using `fetch()` to make the same GET request:
```
fetch('https://example.com/api/data')
  .then(function(response) {
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(function(data) {
    console.log(data); // Success!
  })
  .catch(function(error) {
    console.log('Request failed', error);
  });
```
Both of these examples make a GET request to the specified URL and log the response to the console. You can use the `open()` method to specify a different HTTP method (such as POST or PUT) and the `send()` method to send data along with the request. You can also use the `setRequestHeader()` method to set request headers, such as `Content-Type` or `Authorization`.

For more information and examples, you can refer to the documentation for `XMLHttpRequest` and `fetch()`.

